# MCP Browser Client Project Structure

MCP Browser Client is a system for testing MCP (Model Context Protocol) client libraries in the browser and building applications on them, centered on the agent loop: a model choosing tools and using them. Everything runs in a web page and a service worker shared by every tab, delivered as a static site, with nothing to install or configure locally.

## Core Components

### MCP client libraries (pluggable)
- Registry and loaders: [public/mcp-clients.js](mdc:public/mcp-clients.js) (`MCP_CLIENTS`, `DEFAULT_CLIENT`, `loadMcpClient`)
- The worker's active library: [public/client-runtime.js](mdc:public/client-runtime.js) (`setClient`, `ensureClient`, ...); the choice persists in Cache Storage
- TypeScript SDK library (`sdk`, default): [sdk-client/index.js](mdc:sdk-client/index.js), an adapter over `@modelcontextprotocol/client`
- Rust/WASM library (`wasm`): [src/lib.rs](mdc:src/lib.rs) exports; protocol in `src/mcp/`, sign-in in `src/oauth/`
- Every library implements the same interface (JSON strings in and out, JSON `McpError` rejections), documented in [DEVELOPMENT.md](mdc:DEVELOPMENT.md)

### Service Worker
- Implementation: [public/sw.js](mdc:public/sw.js)
  - Routes page messages to the active library (`mcpClient`); never builds JSON-RPC itself
  - Owns sign-in storage (`authStore.js`) and run history (`workbench/store.js`)
  - Runs only the calls pages send (`handleToolCall`), each with its own server's token; it keeps no server list

### Web Interface
- Page: [public/index.html](mdc:public/index.html)
 - The top bar (Runtime menu with the library picker `#clientSelect`), the layout, the Apps page (the app builder, and its start page with the examples) and the Guide
 - `AppShell`, the controller: talks to the service worker, owns servers, sign-in, tool calls, resource reads and prompt gets, and emits events instead of drawing UI
- Workbench: [public/workbench/](mdc:public/workbench/index.js)
  - One custom element per part of the screen in `components/` (rail, server bar, tools with the Resources and Prompts tabs, request, resource, prompt, response, contents, splitter, dock, sheet, palette)
  - Shared state in `workbench.js`; the layout is grid areas in `workbench.css`, sized by the splitters' CSS variables
  - Resource templates' URIs in `uri-template.js` (RFC 6570); Pre-fill's test data in `prefill.js`
- App builder: [public/apps/](mdc:public/apps/index.js)
 - An app is a screen (components, among them HTML parts a model or a tool makes, or HTML) and a flow (rules: when, call, then); the state is `apps.js` (with the model the builder asks and the examples, `chatApp` and `dashboardApp`), the components are in `components/`: rail and start page (`rail.js`), header, the canvas (`canvas.js`) and its Inspector, the Outline's Screen, Flow and Try it, and the editors they share (`rule-editor.js`, `html-source.js`, `call-editor.js`, `model-picker.js`, `trace.js`)
 - `runner.js` runs an app: its screen in a sandboxed frame (`frameRuntime`, `frameDocument`), its flow in the page, each call through `AppShell.runTool` with source `app`, and the agent loop: the tool calls in a model's answer, with source `reply`
 - `flow.js` (triggers, templates, arguments, the JSON in an answer, problems, sentences), `graph.js` (the flow as ports and wires), `boxes.js` (what boxes and conversations show, the request a rule adds to its prompt, drawing answers), `agent.js` (what a model is told about your tools, the calls in its answer, the limit) and `screen.js` (components and parts to HTML) are pure but for `sanitizePart`; `ask.js` asks the model for HTML; `dml.js` reads and writes the DML an app downloads as, `zip.js` the zip, `store.js` keeps apps in IndexedDB `mcp_apps`

## Build System
- `npm run build` builds every library: `npm run build:sdk` ([sdk-client/build.mjs](mdc:sdk-client/build.mjs), esbuild) and `npm run build:wasm` ([wasm-build.sh](mdc:wasm-build.sh), [Cargo.toml](mdc:Cargo.toml))
- Each build writes a hash file (`public/build.js`, `public/build-sdk.js`) that `mcp-clients.js` imports, so a rebuild is a worker update

## Testing and measuring
- `npm run test:browser` (the default library; `--client=sdk|wasm`, `--reference`), `npm run test:browser:wasm`, `npm run test:rust`
- `npm run test:unit` ([tests/prefill.test.mjs](mdc:tests/prefill.test.mjs), [tests/uri-template.test.mjs](mdc:tests/uri-template.test.mjs), [tests/apps.test.mjs](mdc:tests/apps.test.mjs)): Pre-fill's test data from [public/workbench/prefill.js](mdc:public/workbench/prefill.js), validated with Ajv, resource template URIs against RFC 6570's examples, and the app builder's DML, zips, flow, graph, parts, examples and agent loop
- `npm run bench` ([tests/bench.mjs](mdc:tests/bench.mjs), [public/bench/](mdc:public/bench/index.html)): tool-call throughput per library

## Naming
- Say "MCP client library" (or "library") for the pluggable protocol implementation, and use `client` in code: `set_client`, `client_loaded`, `client_status`, `?client=`, `--client=`
- The agent loop says model (an MCP tool that answers, `Apps.model()`, with its `messageField` and `conversationField`), conversation (an output with `show: 'conversation'`, whose entries say who they're from), instructions and tools (a rule's `instructions` and `tools`), and reply (a tool call in a model's answer, a run with source `reply`). There is no Chat app any more, only the Chat example; the Chat app's names only survive in the code that removes its old stored data
- The app builder says app, example (what Chat and Project pulse are), screen, component, element (anything with an id), part (HTML a model or a tool made, whose elements are `part.id`), flow, rule (`when`, `call`, `then`), trigger (one `when`), route (one `then`; as it's sent, if it works, if it fails), box (an output that says what it shows and what goes in it), the prompt (the call's field a model's prompt goes in, which a rule's instructions, tools and boxes add to) and the request (what the boxes add to it), and DML (the markup an app downloads as, `app.dml`). On the canvas: port, wire (one part of a rule), Start, Sent, transform (what a route shows), Library and Inspector
